WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.480
Black Panther is already being hailed as an
astonishing installment to the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.080
Universe, with tight direction, top-of-the-line
performances, and stunning action sequences.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.000
The studio's had no trouble commanding an
audience over the last decade, of course,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.380
but with Black Panther, it looks like they're
delivering a truly special cinematic experience,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.580
and here's why.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.440
Behind the lens talent

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.120
Black Panther might only be director Ryan
Coogler's third feature film, but there are

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.100
plenty of reasons Marvel trusted him to kick
off its all-new franchise.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.740
For starters, even though his filmography
is limited, it's still quite impressive.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.560
His first feature, Fruitvale Station, won
the Grand Jury Prize at Sundance and garnered

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.820
near-universal acclaim.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.620
And his second movie, Creed, instantly became
known as one of the best entries in the Rocky

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.840
series, and cleaned up at the box office,
too.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:51.480
Perhaps most importantly, he also just so
happens to be a lifelong Black Panther superfan,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.660
which was a big factor in the hiring process.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.160
Marvel Studios head honcho Kevin Feige told
Entertainment Weekly:

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.540
"[Coogler made the film] for his 8-year old
self."

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.200
"Most importantly, you do it for other 8-year-olds,
to inspire the next generation the way we

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:04.880
were inspired.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.420
And in this case, when Ryan was growing up,
perhaps there weren't that many of these heroes

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.360
to be inspired by that looked like him."

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.420
It appears that having someone with a real
heart for the art at the helm obviously paid

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:15.920
off here.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.580
A true leading man

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.440
T'Challa was introduced to film audiences
in 2016's Captain America: Civil War, a movie

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.220
already stuffed to the brim with heroes.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.920
Even though the movie had a frenetic pace
and tons of heroes to deal with, Chadwick

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.220
Boseman's regal performance really sold audiences
on the Wakandan superhero.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.320
And in Black Panther, he steadily commands
the screen as the rousing royal.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.260
"I am ready."

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.720
Perhaps it's no surprise that Boseman is impressing
audiences in the film.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.680
After all, he'd already been a cinematic force
with leading roles in movies like 42 and Get

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.180
On Up.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.980
Compelling villainy

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.220
No Ryan Coogler film would be complete without
the director's muse, Michael B. Jordan.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.420
The actor starred in both of Coogler's prior
pictures, and this time, he's getting to do

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.780
something totally new.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.580
Jordan stars in Black Panther as Eric Killmonger,
an exiled Wakandan-turned-U.S. Black Ops soldier

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.980
who returns to challenge T'Challa for the
throne.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.360
As with his last two Coogler collaborations,
Jordan's work in the film is already being

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.300
hailed as "the best MCU villain since Loki".

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.980
"I'mma burn it all."

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.200
Considering how much trouble Marvel movies
have had making satisfying screen baddies

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.420
lately, that's high praise for the red-hot
actor's work in the movie.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.020
Scene stealers

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.900
With all of the A-listers starring in Black
Panther it might be hard to believe that an

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.880
up-and-comer has emerged as the scene-stealer
of the whole film.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.420
But that's exactly what happened with Letitia
Wright as Shuri.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.420
"WOO!

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:37.920
Let's go!"

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.700
You might recognize Wright as Renie in Humans
or as Nish in Black Mirror's "Black Museum"

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:43.200
episode.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.640
But after Black Panther, she's bound to be
as big of a star as anyone else on the film's

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:48.140
roster.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.540
The character is brilliant, spritely, and
a complete standout that'll definitely make

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.460
audiences smile.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.100
And Shuri isn't the only one with some major
girl power in Black Panther.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.660
The characters Okoye and Nakia, played by
Danai Gurira and Lupita Nyong'o respectively,

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.700
have also received high marks for badassery.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:06.760
Mood music

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.000
If the filmmaking and acting talent involved
in the film wasn't impressive enough, the

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.040
film's soundtrack is also brimming with exciting
voices.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.190
Kendrick Lamar produced the album and managed
to round up hip-hop favorites like Schoolboy

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:24.900
Q, Vince Staples, Future, The Weeknd, and
SZA to contribute tracks alongside Lamar himself.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.960
Marvel's Guardians of the Galaxy soundtracks
might've made waves for reviving some old

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.680
favorites, but Black Panther is taking things
a step further with an original album that's

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:34.920
already got people talking.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.220
Action satisfaction

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.459
It just wouldn't be a Marvel movie without
a ton of action-packed set pieces, and Black

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.340
Panther delivers on that angle just as well
as everywhere else.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.760
The film's fight scenes have drawn comparisons
to slick action fare like the Kingsmen and

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:54.379
James Bond series for their sophistication
levels, and there's at least one massive chase

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.480
scene that should get audiences' adrenaline
properly pumping as well.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.600
Post-credit impact

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.800
Another signature Marvel move is to include
post-credit sequences, and the two featured

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.220
in Black Panther seem to be signals for what's
ahead.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.700
Without spoiling them, let's just say that
they point to a brave new future for the Kingdom

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:16.739
of Wakanda and connect the dots between prior
Marvel films and what's to come with Avengers:

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.340
Infinity War.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.180
Cultural pride

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.740
It's no secret that Western films have a history
of portraying African nations as being impoverished

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:25.240
and violent.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.979
However, with a mainstream film like this
one showcasing more of the vibrant and beautiful

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:34.169
aspects of African culture, the film has the
potential to change the way Hollywood treats

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:35.320
the birthplace of humanity.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.040
As Lupita Nyong'o, who was raised in Kenya,
put it:

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.980
"...Marvel has a way of really affecting popular
culture, and to have that popular culture

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:46.160
informed with things that are of African origin
… is powerful.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.580
Hopefully it changes the general idea of what
being an African is.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.200
Too often times we see Africa as a place that
is wanting, and here it's a place that you

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.200
want to go."

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.340
So, beyond just being entertainment, Black
Panther could very well represent a moment

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.960
of cultural awakening altogether.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.020
The way that the film's talent celebrated
native fashion at the premiere, it seems to

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.140
have already arrived as a source of pride.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.440
Not only that, but the movie could also re-shape
the way studios think about black cinema as

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:12.940
a whole.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.260
Lately, black directors have been having more
and more success getting their stories onto

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:17.760
screens.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:23.140
Coogler's contemporaries such as Ava DuVernay
and Jordan Peele have been steadily proving

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:27.860
that cinematic stories told from a black cultural
viewpoint can not only give young black audiences

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.000
the kind of representation they've been yearning
for but also generate boatloads of cash.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.860
Perhaps it'll take a pop culture titan like
Marvel to drive this point home once and for

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:40.820
all, but if it does, Black Panther could be
a serious game changer.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.420
Thanks for watching!

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.140
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.300
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

